{"tool_name":"tmhmm","contents":["oeb","bioschemas","biotools"],"fetched_metadata":{"biotools":{"id":"tmhmm","home":"http://cbs.dtu.dk/services/TMHMM/","license":"Other","summary":"Prediction of transmembrane helices in proteins.","addition_date":"2015-02-16T20:23:33Z","last_update_date":"2019-07-19T07:48:11Z","tool_type":["Command-line tool","Web application","Web service"],"version":["2.0"],"collections":["Proteomics"],"biotools_curie":"biotools:tmhmm","cost":"Free of charge (with restrictions)","credit":[{"email":"akrogh@bio.ku.dk","name":"Anders Krogh","orcidid":"http://orcid.org/0000-0002-5147-6282"},{"name":"CBS","typeEntity":"Institute","typeRole":["Provider"]},{"typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Primary contact"],"url":"http://www.bioinformatics.dtu.dk/english/Service/Contact"}],"edit_permission":{"authors":["proteomics.bio.tools","veits@bmb.sdu.dk"],"type":"group"},"function":[{"input":[{"data":{"term":"Sequence","uri":"http://edamontology.org/data_2044"},"format":[{"term":"FASTA","uri":"http://edamontology.org/format_1929"}]}],"note":"Prediction of transmembrane helices in proteins","operation":[{"term":"Transmembrane protein prediction","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_0269"}],"output":[{"data":{"term":"Protein features","uri":"http://edamontology.org/data_1277"},"format":[{"term":"GFF","uri":"http://edamontology.org/format_2305"}]},{"data":{"term":"Sequence report","uri":"http://edamontology.org/data_2955"},"format":[{"term":"Binary format","uri":"http://edamontology.org/format_2333"}]}]}],"maturity":"Emerging","name":"TMHMM","operating_system":["Linux","Mac","Windows"],"owner":"CBS","publication":[{"doi":"10.1006/jmbi.2000.4315","metadata":{"abstract":"We describe and validate a new membrane protein topology prediction method, TMHMM, based on a hidden Markov model. We present a detailed analysis of TMHMM's performance, and show that it correctly predicts 97-98% of the transmembrane helices. Additionally, TMHMM can discriminate between soluble and membrane proteins with both specificity and sensitivity better than 99%, although the accuracy drops when signal peptides are present. This high degree of accuracy allowed us to predict reliably integral membrane proteins in a large collection of genomes. Based on these predictions, we estimate that 20-30% of all genes in most genomes encode membrane proteins, which is in agreement with previous estimates. We further discovered that proteins with Nin-Cin topologies are strongly preferred in all examined organisms, except Caenorhabditis elegans, where the large number of 7TM receptors increases the counts for Nout-Cin topologies. We discuss the possible relevance of this finding for our understanding of membrane protein assembly mechanisms. \u00a9 2001 Academic Press.","authors":[{"name":"Krogh A."},{"name":"Larsson B."},{"name":"Sonnhammer E.L.L."},{"name":"Von Heijne G."}],"citationCount":8082,"date":"2001-01-19T00:00:00Z","journal":"Journal of Molecular Biology","title":"Predicting transmembrane protein topology with a hidden Markov model: Application to complete genomes"},"pmid":"11152613","type":["Primary"]}],"topic":[{"term":"Membrane and lipoproteins","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_0820"},{"term":"Protein folds and structural domains","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_0736"},{"term":"Protein modifications","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_0601"},{"term":"Protein secondary structure","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3542"},{"term":"Proteomics","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_0121"}],"validated":1},"bioschemas":{"name":"TMHMM","home":"https://bio.tools/tmhmm","license":"Other","version":"2.0","summary":"Prediction of transmembrane helices in proteins.","tool_type":"sc:SoftwareApplication"},"oeb":{"metrics":[{"@id":"https://openebench.bsc.es/monitor/metrics/biotools:tmhmm:2.0/cmd/cbs.dtu.dk","@license":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","@nmsp":"biotools","@type":"metrics","deprecated":true,"project":{"build":{},"deployment":{}